  message: "## Deep Researcher Prompt — Curriculum Generation\n\nThe following prompt is designed to be given to a naive AI agent with web search capability. It provides enough context about hypergumbo and the bakeoff system for the agent to produce useful curricula without having seen the codebase.\n\n---\n\n### PROMPT\n\nYou are designing test curricula for **hypergumbo**, a local-first CLI tool that analyzes codebases and produces a \"repo behavior map\" — a JSON graph of symbols (functions, classes, routes), call edges, framework patterns, and supply-chain tiers. It works by parsing source code with tree-sitter (no runtime execution, no LSP, no network), so its quality depends entirely on how well its per-language analyzer handles that language's idioms.\n\n**Your task:** For each of the 5 languages below, produce a bakeoff curriculum: a sequence of 2-4 cohorts, each containing 2-4 real open-source repos, ordered from simple to complex. Each curriculum will be used to test hypergumbo's analyzer for that language and surface gaps.\n\n**What hypergumbo measures per repo:**\n- Symbol extraction (functions, methods, classes, structs, interfaces, traits)\n- Call graph edges (which function calls which, including cross-file resolution)\n- Framework/route detection (HTTP endpoints, RPC handlers, lifecycle callbacks)\n- Supply-chain tier classification (first-party vs dependency vs transitive)\n- Forward/reverse slicing (\"what depends on X\" / \"what does X depend on\")\n- IO boundary detection (file, network, subprocess, IPC operations)\n- Interface/trait dispatch (which concrete types implement which interfaces)\n\n**What makes a good curriculum repo:**\n- Open-source, well-known, actively maintained\n- Shallow-clonable from GitHub (ideally <100MB)\n- Exercises the language's distinctive idioms and patterns\n- Has a clear architectural structure (not just a single file)\n- Represents a category that real developers work on (web server, CLI tool, library, data pipeline)\n\n**For each language, think about:**\n1. What are the language's distinctive patterns that a static analyzer might struggle with? (e.g., Swift protocol extensions, Erlang OTP behaviors, Haskell type classes, Scala implicits, Obj-C message passing)\n2. What are the most popular frameworks in that language? (e.g., Vapor/SwiftUI for Swift, Akka/Play for Scala)\n3. What repo archetypes exist? (CLI tool, web server, library, mobile app, data pipeline)\n4. How should cohorts progress? (Start with small/simple repos that test basic symbol extraction, end with large/complex repos that test framework detection and cross-module resolution)\n\n**The 5 languages and why they're underserved:**\n\n| Language | Current State | Key Gaps to Test |\n|----------|--------------|-----------------|\n| **Swift** | 589 LOC, 39 tests, no framework detection, no type inference | Protocol extensions, SwiftUI patterns, Vapor routes, async/await, property wrappers |\n| **Objective-C** | 10 commits, same as config parsers | Message passing (objc_msgSend), categories, protocols, delegate patterns, UIKit lifecycle |\n| **Erlang** | 8 commits, below shader languages | OTP behaviors (gen_server, supervisor), pattern matching dispatch, hot code reload, message passing |\n| **Haskell** | 6 commits, same as Elm | Type classes, monad transformers, do-notation desugaring, qualified imports, Servant/Yesod routes |\n| **Scala** | 16 commits, below Groovy despite wider deployment | Implicits/givens, trait linearization, pattern matching, Akka actors, Play Framework routes, SBT build |\n\n**Output format for each language:**\n\n```markdown\n# Curriculum: <Language> DEEP Bakeoff\n\n## Rationale\n<2-3 sentences on why these repos were chosen and what they test>\n\n## Cohorts\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\n# Cohort 1 — <theme> (simple, tests basic extraction)\n./scripts/bakeoff-features cohort --repos <repo1>,<repo2>\n\n# Cohort 2 — <theme> (medium, tests framework detection)\n./scripts/bakeoff-features cohort --repos <repo1>,<repo2>,<repo3>\n\n# Cohort 3 — <theme> (complex, tests cross-module resolution)\n./scripts/bakeoff-features  # efcf
    cohort --repos <repo1>,<repo2>\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Per-Cohort Notes\n### Cohort 1 — <theme>\n- <repo1> (~<size>MB, <license>): <1 sentence on what it is and what pattern it tests>\n  Clone: <github URL>\n- <repo2> ...\n\n### Cohort 2 — <theme>\n...\n```\n\n**Important constraints:**\n- Use REAL repos that currently exist on GitHub. Verify they are actively maintained (last commit within 12 months).\n- Prefer repos between 1-100MB. Avoid repos >200MB.\n- Each cohort should have a clear theme (e.g., \"CLI tools\", \"web frameworks\", \"data libraries\").\n- Include clone URLs for every repo.\n- Note the primary license for each repo.\n\nPlease produce all 5 curricula."  # efcf
